<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Galloway</h1><p class="st-meta">1110 CE – 1234 CE (approx.) · capital Kirkcudbright</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A semi-independent Gaelic lordship in south-west Scotland, whose lords acted almost as kings until the line ended in 1234.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Galloway takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norse-gaels">Gall-Gaidheil</a>, the 'foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a>' of mixed Norse and Gaelic descent who settled the region in the 9th and 10th centuries. In the early 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergus-of-galloway">Fergus of Galloway</a> emerged as its ruler, styled king in some sources, and married an illegitimate daughter of Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords of Galloway ruled a land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sea-power with its own laws, while founding monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dundrennan-abbey">Dundrennan</a> and Soulseat and reviving the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whithorn">Whithorn</a>. Fergus's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roland-lord-of-galloway">Roland</a> and great-grandson Alan were great Anglo-Scottish nobles; Alan's fleet raided Ireland and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isles">Kingdom of the Isles</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Alan died in 1234 without a legitimate son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> divided Galloway between his daughters, crushing a revolt. The lordship became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland</a>; Alan's grandson was the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> Balliol.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-galloway.jpg" alt="Lordship of Galloway" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronet_of_a_British_Baron.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1110 CE – 1234 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kirkcudbright</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fergus of Galloway, Roland, Alan of Galloway</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaelic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Galloway, Kingdom of the Gall-Gaidheil</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1172&amp;focus=lordship-of-galloway" aria-label="Lordship of Galloway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4591 302 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Galloway lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4911 558L4892 500"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4892 500h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4911 558h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4911 558h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kirkcudbright. <a href="/atlas?year=1172&amp;focus=lordship-of-galloway">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
